import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const build = `cargo check --workspace                                     # quick build
cargo check --target wasm32-unknown-unknown -p tuile-core   # the wasm guardrail
cargo test --workspace
cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings`;

const tests = `cargo test --workspace > /tmp/tests.log 2>&1
grep -E "(test result|FAILED|panicked)" /tmp/tests.log`;

const viewer = `cargo run -p wgpu-viewer -- fixtures/tileset-simple/tileset.json`;

const doc = `cargo doc --workspace --no-deps`;

const site = `cd site
npm install
npm run docs:api     # cargo doc --workspace --no-deps, staged under /docs/api
npm run build        # static build → dist/
npm run preview      # serve dist/ locally`;
---

<Base
  title="Getting started — Tuile"
  description="Build, test and run the Tuile workspace: the real cargo commands, the WebAssembly guardrail, and how to build and serve this site."
  kicker="Getting started"
  heading="Build it, check it, run it."
  lede="Tuile is a plain Cargo workspace. Rust 1.91 or newer, edition 2021, no build system on top, no code generation step to remember."
>
  <h2>Build and check</h2>

  <pre><code>{build}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The second line is the one that matters most. <code>tuile-core</code> compiling to
    <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code> is the cheapest available proof that no renderer, no
    async runtime and no HTTP framework has crept into the core. Add the target once with
    <code>rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The workspace denies <code>unsafe</code> outright and warns on <code>unwrap</code> and
    <code>panic</code>, so a clean clippy run means something here.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading test output</h2>

  <p>
    One local convention worth adopting, because it has already cost time: never pipe a
    test, build or lint run through <code>head</code> or <code>tail</code>.
    <code>head</code> closes the pipe as soon as it has its lines and kills the run partway
    through, so the summary line never arrives and a truncated run is indistinguishable from
    a hang. <code>tail</code> shows nothing at all until the process exits. Redirect the
    whole thing and search it afterwards.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{tests}</code></pre>

  <p class="note">
    And a green suite is not proof. Write the test in the same change as the behaviour, then
    revert the behaviour and watch the test fail. Say plainly what a test does
    <em>not</em> cover, rather than letting a passing run imply coverage it does not have.
  </p>

  <h2>Running a viewer</h2>

  <pre><code>{viewer}</code></pre>

  <p class="note">
    <strong>Two honest caveats.</strong> That is the command the repository's contributor
    guide documents, and it is the shape the viewer is meant to settle on. Today the
    <code>wgpu-viewer</code> example in the workspace is the streaming <em>globe</em>
    viewer: it takes no path argument and instead reads a data-provider credential from the
    environment, so it will refuse to start without one. And the <code>fixtures/</code>
    directory named above is not committed yet. The viewer's own module documentation, in
    the <a href="/docs">API reference</a>, carries the current controls and environment
    variables — including the ones that record a camera path and fly it again exactly.
  </p>

  <p>
    Drag to orbit, right-drag to pan, wheel to zoom. The browser builds of the same engine
    live under <code>examples/wasm-globe</code> and <code>examples/web-viewer</code>; the
    headless harnesses — bulk pre-fetching, cache warming, and a probe that reports what a
    traversal actually selected — live beside them.
  </p>

  <h2>Generating the API documentation</h2>

  <pre><code>{doc}</code></pre>

  <p>
    That writes rustdoc for every crate in the workspace into <code>target/doc</code>. The
    <a href="/docs">API reference</a> page explains how this site serves it.
  </p>

  <h2>Building this site</h2>

  <p>
    The site is an Astro application built statically: the build produces a folder of
    files, the generated rustdoc among them, and no server of its own.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{site}</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>npm run docs:api</code> runs the rustdoc build above and stages the output where
    the site can serve it; it is not part of <code>npm run build</code>, because a
    documentation build of the whole workspace takes minutes and a copy edit should not pay
    for it. <code>npm run build:all</code> runs both in order. Nothing generated is
    committed.
  </p>

